{
  "id": "py-15-for-range",
  "track": "python",
  "unit": "py-u3",
  "order": 15,
  "title": "for loops and range()",
  "summary": "Run a block once for every number in a range, without managing a counter yourself.",
  "language": "python",
  "minutes": 20,
  "visual": "memory",
  "visuals": [
    "loop",
    "memory"
  ],
  "intro": {
    "text": "A `for` loop runs its block once for **each item** in something, and gives you the item in a variable:\n\n```\nfor n in range(3):\n    print(n)\n```\n\nprints 0, 1 and 2. `range(3)` gives three numbers, **starting at 0 and stopping before 3**. The stop number is never included.\n\n- `range(stop)`: 0 up to stop - 1\n- `range(start, stop)`: start up to stop - 1\n- `range(start, stop, step)`: jump by step; a negative step counts down\n\nThere is no counter to forget, so no infinite loop. `range(n)` is the easy way to repeat something n times. The loop panel shows the value of the loop variable in each round.",
    "analogy": "A for loop is like dealing cards: you take the cards one at a time from the top of the deck until it is empty. range(5) is a deck with the cards 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4 in order."
  },
